WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.545
Welcome everyone.

00:00:07.545 --> 00:00:11.100
I'm delighted to introduce you to Jason Picard,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.580
who is the Founding Assistant Professor of Vietnamese History and

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.480
Culture at VinUniversity in Hanoi, Vietnam.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.420
In addition to hosting an MA from Cornell and a PhD in History from Berkeley,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.390
Jason has lived and worked for extended periods in

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:31.140
Vietnam and is quite possibly more familiar with

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.200
the Vietnamese archives and the currents and trends of

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.725
Vietnamese history and historiography than any other scholar working today,

00:00:39.725 --> 00:00:41.554
no matter the place.

00:00:41.554 --> 00:00:44.120
I could say this based on experience.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.750
Literally, every time I have a question about Vietnamese history,

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.890
I must apologize to Jason for this because he must think I sometimes,

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.220
whenever I have a question, I just reach out to him for an answer.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.280
But especially every time I have a question about Vietnamese history,

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.695
especially 20th century political,

00:01:00.695 --> 00:01:03.070
intellectual or social history,

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.745
Jason always has the answer.

00:01:05.745 --> 00:01:08.044
When I say has the answer,

00:01:08.044 --> 00:01:11.240
these aren't just off the cuff speculative answers,

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.095
the kind of thing that we all do when we're talking about academic ideas,

00:01:15.095 --> 00:01:17.455
but they're always grounded fact-filled,

00:01:17.455 --> 00:01:20.480
evidence-based answers that demonstrate

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.770
an intimate knowledge of literature in Vietnamese and in English,

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:29.240
in French, as well as source materials from the Vietnamese archives,

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:33.275
alternative archives, conversations he's had on the street,

00:01:33.275 --> 00:01:34.610
people he knows,

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.120
other scholars that he can direct you to.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.380
I mean, this is serious understanding and deep knowledge of Vietnamese history.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.590
It's a real pleasure to have known Jason for

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:48.935
many years since he was doing his MA at Cornell.

00:01:48.935 --> 00:01:55.505
It's been a real inspiration to just learn with him on this journey into Vietnam studies.

00:01:55.505 --> 00:01:58.490
He's the author of some truly important works,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.845
including Fertile Lands Await,

00:02:00.845 --> 00:02:06.625
The Promises and Pitfalls of Directed Resettlement, 1954 through 1958.

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:09.120
Also Renegade the story of

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:15.440
South Vietnam's First National Opposition Newspaper, 1955 through 1958.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.275
Both of those are in the Journal of Vietnamese Studies.

00:02:18.275 --> 00:02:22.040
I also encourage you to really mine all of Jason's writing,

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.770
some of his book reviews will actually end

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.070
up as historiographical essays in their own right.

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:32.450
Also his MA thesis and PhD dissertation as well,

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.270
really remarkable works of scholarship.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:40.700
Jason is currently completing a book manuscript entitled Fragmented Loyalties.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.740
How Vietnam's great migration destabilized a nation and altered a war,

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.360
which examines the impact of one of

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.765
the most crucial events of the Vietnam's 20th century,

00:02:50.765 --> 00:02:54.845
the migration of 1954 through 1955.

00:02:54.845 --> 00:02:59.330
But please join me in welcoming Jason for today's talk,

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:02.300
Vietnam's Machiavelli Lê Đức Thọ and

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.815
his quest to shape the Communist Party of Vietnam. Jason.

00:03:06.815 --> 00:03:09.650
Great. Erik, thank you so much.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.630
That was quite a welcome.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:17.065
Now I feel almost like I should just feel questions after that.

00:03:17.065 --> 00:03:22.330
It's a real pleasure to be here despite the late hour in Vietnam.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:24.880
I recognize some people out there.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.950
It's great to see you.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.750
It's been a very very long time.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.880
There is a lot of material that I have to

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.895
cover and I'm obviously not going to cover it all.

00:03:36.895 --> 00:03:44.055
I just wanted to start by discussing just briefly how I came to this topic.

00:03:44.055 --> 00:03:47.280
About six years ago,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:48.690
I had an opportunity,

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:52.380
after I had finished my dissertation to read

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:58.005
a book called The Winning Side, [inaudible] in Vietnamese.

00:03:58.005 --> 00:04:01.640
I was struck by the character Lê Đức Thọ.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.580
I think most of you will know the name Lê Đức Thọ

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:08.555
from the Paris Peace Conference with Henry Kissinger.

00:04:08.555 --> 00:04:11.960
Tho was Kissinger's arch nemesis there.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:16.220
Some of you may also know his name as the lone soul

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.950
who refused the Nobel Peace Prize back in 1973.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.390
But by and large in the West,

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:25.415
that's about all that we know about him.

00:04:25.415 --> 00:04:30.350
Recently some scholars have written more about this.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:35.480
They've dealt in a bit more about his role in Vietnam;

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.820
his role in the Communist Party.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.230
But by and large,

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.930
he is an enigma.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.315
As I read this book,

00:04:44.315 --> 00:04:48.470
as I said six or so years ago, it struck me.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:51.980
The role that he had as

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:59.140
a seminal figure in the party was remarkable and yet very few people are aware of it.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.685
The reason I say this,

00:05:01.685 --> 00:05:04.310
the seminal role is because,

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:11.300
as you guys will see, his role was as the head for about 35 plus years.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:18.160
The head of the Central Organization of the Communist Party of Vietnam.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:25.040
Some of you may be familiar with old Soviet history, with Chinese history.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.830
The Central Organization of the Communist Party was in all of these countries.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:35.600
It was in charge of what's known as the nomenklatura,

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.630
and nomenklatura was basically the list of names.

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:45.605
It was the list of all of the party officials with all members.

00:05:45.605 --> 00:05:48.970
We're talking in the case of Vietnam, millions of members.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:53.914
This Central Organization Commission was in charge of employment,

00:05:53.914 --> 00:05:58.735
moving people around, assigning people to jobs.

00:05:58.735 --> 00:06:02.390
This might seem like a mundane task,

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:05.825
but it also meant that Lê Đức Thọ,

00:06:05.825 --> 00:06:08.090
as the chief of this organization,

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:12.550
was also holding the levers of power.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:14.450
As I thought about it more and more,

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:15.875
I realized, my goodness,

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:24.145
I know next to nothing about how power actually functions in the Vietnamese party.

00:06:24.145 --> 00:06:27.310
In recent years, we've had some wonderful books.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:33.870
I think many of you may be familiar with Dang's book on Ideology Within the Party,

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.040
which is a brilliant book.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.310
I would recommend it to anyone and everyone.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:43.515
[inaudible] recently published his book with Hawaii.

00:06:43.515 --> 00:06:47.760
Again, a brilliant book and I would recommend it to everyone.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:51.990
I'm still left, all these many years later,

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.125
many of you will recognize David Marr,

00:06:54.125 --> 00:06:58.680
his book 1945, Quest for Power.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:02.480
I'm still to this day left with the question of how

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:07.685
does power exists within the Communist Party?

00:07:07.685 --> 00:07:12.365
What role do things like relationships play?

00:07:12.365 --> 00:07:15.770
This is something, over the last few years,

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:18.965
has really aided me.

00:07:18.965 --> 00:07:20.900
About a year or so ago,

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.390
I just started to play around with Lê Đức Thọ.

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:27.565
I started collecting some memoirs that I had found.

00:07:27.565 --> 00:07:35.690
There are some very famous ones by [inaudible] and several other people,

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:39.050
[inaudible], and [inaudible].

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:40.610
Some of you may be familiar with them,

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:42.305
if not, not a big deal.

00:07:42.305 --> 00:07:46.010
But anyway, I just got so wrapped up in this.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:48.890
I pushed aside the other work I should be doing,

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:54.585
and I just decided I'm going to start delving into Lê Đức Thọ. Who he was?

00:07:54.585 --> 00:07:56.935
What made him tick?

00:07:56.935 --> 00:07:59.035
That was basically what I wanted to know.

00:07:59.035 --> 00:08:03.770
As I came to learn more about the levels of power that he did

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:09.365
hold as the chief of this Central Organization Commission,

00:08:09.365 --> 00:08:15.170
I realized the power that he wielded was in

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:21.395
so many ways enough that he was shaping the party and the direction it would cover,

00:08:21.395 --> 00:08:22.790
even up to today,

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:24.380
which I'll discuss at the very end.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:26.270
I won't have very much time,

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:30.695
but I'd like to conclude with a little discussion about

00:08:30.695 --> 00:08:35.585
his role in shaping Doi Moi renovation 1986.

00:08:35.585 --> 00:08:41.655
That's just how I got to Lê Đức Thọ.

00:08:41.655 --> 00:08:44.215
I hope this is working. There we go.

00:08:44.215 --> 00:08:47.210
As I said, all of you probably recognize this.

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:51.680
This is a very famous image of Lê Đức Thọ standing next to Henry Kissinger

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:59.484
in 1972/'73 with the signing of the Paris Peace Accords.

00:08:59.484 --> 00:09:02.590
Obviously, he's quite famous for this.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:07.750
Something else many of you may be familiar with is the works of Duong Thu Huong.

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:12.700
Duong Thu Huong is a Vietnamese novelist writer.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:16.570
She lives in Paris.

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:20.860
No longer welcome in Vietnam, sadly.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:25.990
But she's written many books about Vietnam obviously,

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:27.925
but in particular,

00:09:27.925 --> 00:09:30.625
examining the Communist Party.

00:09:30.625 --> 00:09:32.050
One of her more recent books,

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:35.980
The Zenith being [inaudible] in Vietnamese,

00:09:35.980 --> 00:09:41.830
examines the Part B and her description of the Communist Party at the elite levels of

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:47.710
the very top is one that is extremely bleak.

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:50.710
But the reason I have this image of The Zenith,

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:54.610
and the reason I think it's so important that I mention this,

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:59.935
is that her depiction of Lê Đức Thọ is of particular interest here.

00:09:59.935 --> 00:10:04.105
In this book, Lê Đức Thọ is weak.

00:10:04.105 --> 00:10:05.845
She doesn't use the name Lê Đức Thọ,

00:10:05.845 --> 00:10:07.615
she uses the name Sau.

00:10:07.615 --> 00:10:14.155
Anh Sau Is actually Lê Đức Thọ's nickname within the party.

00:10:14.155 --> 00:10:17.065
Anh Sau meaning brother number 6.

00:10:17.065 --> 00:10:21.250
In this book, there is a figure Six,

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:27.010
who is in charge of the Central Organization Commission.

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:31.105
What makes her depiction of this so remarkable,

00:10:31.105 --> 00:10:35.605
is that she reveal something that is whispered within Vietnam,

00:10:35.605 --> 00:10:42.415
which is that Lê Đức Thọ did indeed try to have Ho Chi Minh murdered.

00:10:42.415 --> 00:10:44.215
There's no evidence for this,

00:10:44.215 --> 00:10:46.525
it's just something that sometimes whispered.

00:10:46.525 --> 00:10:48.640
There is no evidence as I said for this,

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:52.030
but nonetheless, it's there in this novel.

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:55.510
For those people who are Vietnamese and who've read it,

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:56.995
it's stunning, Number 1.

00:10:56.995 --> 00:10:59.920
But number 2, the description she gives,

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:05.710
which is that this man Sau orders the lights on Xylem airfield

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:08.440
shut off in the middle of the night as

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:12.175
Ho Chi Minh flight is arriving I cannot remember from where,

00:11:12.175 --> 00:11:15.700
but arriving from somewhere trying to touch down,

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:17.095
the lights are turned off.

00:11:17.095 --> 00:11:19.900
Fortunately, in this novel,

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:24.370
the pilot who's flying

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:30.260
the plane is a seasoned one and he is able to land the plane without trouble.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.535
The reason I'm mentioning this is,

00:11:33.535 --> 00:11:35.740
there is a host of literature.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:40.405
We can talk about these unofficial histories,

00:11:40.405 --> 00:11:43.194
novels, short stories,

00:11:43.194 --> 00:11:51.970
and memoirs that depict Lê Đức Thọ as a brilliant organizer,

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:58.135
a man who played a very central role in the Communist Party for decades,

00:11:58.135 --> 00:12:00.715
but who is ruthless and brutal,

00:12:00.715 --> 00:12:03.820
and thought nothing but of himself.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:07.795
Then there are the official histories.

00:12:07.795 --> 00:12:11.980
The official histories depict a very different figure.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:16.255
Yes, he's a strong figure,

00:12:16.255 --> 00:12:23.020
brilliant organizer, but they'll depict ideas of this very resilient, revolutionary.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:26.620
That's one quote, a brilliant organizer.

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:34.240
I'd love to read you a description of Lê Đức Thọ on the 100th anniversary of his birth,

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:42.475
this is in 2011 in the Communists review of Vietnam.

00:12:42.475 --> 00:12:47.035
They wrote, comrade Lê Đức Thọ was a tireless revolutionary,

00:12:47.035 --> 00:12:49.825
one of Ho Chi Minh's best students.

00:12:49.825 --> 00:12:54.520
He was among the most important cadre of the August revolution,

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:59.245
and a leading revolutionary in the wars against the French and Americans.

00:12:59.245 --> 00:13:00.850
In particular, he devoted

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:07.225
his revolutionary life to building the parties organization apparatus.

00:13:07.225 --> 00:13:10.645
He's described as this brilliant negotiator,

00:13:10.645 --> 00:13:12.640
the man who defeated,

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:17.665
who out-negotiated Henry Kissinger.

00:13:17.665 --> 00:13:25.240
A man who survived the prisons of [inaudible].

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:31.420
These are a depictions of him as this luminary of the Communist Party.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:34.480
This great figure who would not cow to the French,

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:36.775
who would never cow to the Americans,

00:13:36.775 --> 00:13:43.795
and who led the way in terms of organizing the Communist party.

00:13:43.795 --> 00:13:46.600
We have this dichotomy,

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.640
and of course in the West, yes,

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:53.860
we know about him as this great figure in Paris.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:57.925
But beyond that, there's not really that much more known about him.

00:13:57.925 --> 00:14:00.190
Anyway, what I really wanted to do today,

00:14:00.190 --> 00:14:04.150
we have all of this stuff out there.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:07.780
Differing ideas about who Lê Đức Thọ was,

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:13.960
and they all collect little bits of details here and there and they give their spin.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:15.805
I just wanted to ask the question,

00:14:15.805 --> 00:14:17.305
who the heck is this guy?

00:14:17.305 --> 00:14:23.035
What makes him tick? How is it that he was able to amass so much power?

00:14:23.035 --> 00:14:25.630
That's where I really want to work today.

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:28.120
My agenda, If We have time,

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:31.345
I'll try to get through some or all of this.

00:14:31.345 --> 00:14:34.435
My agenda is really to think about him.

00:14:34.435 --> 00:14:36.610
How did he become Lê Đức Thọ?

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:42.955
Second, wanted to think about him as a tiger in the South, which I'll get to.

00:14:42.955 --> 00:14:45.970
Third, the architect of the organization.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:49.945
We in the central organization of the Communist Party and in conclusion,

00:14:49.945 --> 00:14:52.660
If I have some time,

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:56.980
I want to talk about how Tho was able to shape Đổi Mới renovation.

00:14:56.980 --> 00:15:01.120
This image is an amazing image.

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:02.815
I'll get to it a moment,

00:15:02.815 --> 00:15:05.515
we'll come back to it, but this is a remarkable image.

00:15:05.515 --> 00:15:08.350
Anyway, the first section, becoming Lê Đức Thọ.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.270
I want to think about his early years.

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:12.505
who was he? Where did he come from?

00:15:12.505 --> 00:15:18.040
Lê Đức Thọ happened to be born in 1911 in Nam Dinh province in then north

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:24.640
into what we might term a middle-class Nam Dinh family.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:32.425
Now, many official documents would say that he came from a peasant family. He did not.

00:15:32.425 --> 00:15:37.490
His family could trace their heritage back to Hanoi.

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:42.415
I'll just put his name up here for all of you to see, it's relevant.

00:15:42.415 --> 00:15:45.775
Phan Dinh Khai was his birth name.

00:15:45.775 --> 00:15:48.040
Phan Dinh Khai was born in 1911,

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:49.690
as he said in Nam Dinh.

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:54.820
His grandfather was a failed mandarin.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:59.500
He had passed two exams and then failed ultimately,

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:01.525
one of the city exams.

00:16:01.525 --> 00:16:07.735
This would have been in the late 1800s.

00:16:07.735 --> 00:16:15.530
He became an herbal medicinal doctor, pharmacist.

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:20.020
When Phan Dinh Khai was born in 1911,

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:23.650
10 years later he became Phan Dinh Khai's teacher.

00:16:23.650 --> 00:16:29.365
He taught him for about two or three years until he passed away.

00:16:29.365 --> 00:16:32.110
The thing that makes this so interesting,

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:35.409
is that when Phan Dinh Khai was born in 1911,

00:16:35.409 --> 00:16:39.700
it means that by the 1920s he's a teen.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:44.320
Not much is really happening when he is in his very early years,

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:47.995
but his teen years are extremely traumatic.

00:16:47.995 --> 00:16:51.400
Both his grandfather and his father die within

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:56.570
a span of five years between 1923 and then 1928.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:17:01.450
These two men were clearly up until that point,

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:04.345
the two most important figures in his life,

00:17:04.345 --> 00:17:07.045
and their death really struck him.

00:17:07.045 --> 00:17:10.045
What makes us all the more important is,

00:17:10.045 --> 00:17:13.270
as these two deaths occur,

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:17.005
Lê Đức Thọ is living in Nam Dinh.

00:17:17.005 --> 00:17:19.525
Many of you may know,

00:17:19.525 --> 00:17:23.830
at this time in Vietnam,

00:17:23.830 --> 00:17:30.490
in Indochina, there's a lot of seething feeling against the colonial regime.

00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:39.600
There are uprisings and rebellions including at this place, Societe [inaudible].

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.990
This was one of the cotton factories,

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:46.790
one of the clothing factories in Nam Dinh,

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:50.710
in which Lê Đức Thọ had gone to work.

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:54.805
Now, by the time 1928 comes around,

00:17:54.805 --> 00:17:56.785
Lê Đức Thọ has apparently,

00:17:56.785 --> 00:17:59.275
according to official sources,

00:17:59.275 --> 00:18:02.305
he's become a member of

00:18:02.305 --> 00:18:11.835
the revolutionary youthly in Vietnam,

00:18:11.835 --> 00:18:14.400
and a couple years later in 1930,

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:16.440
he allegedly, again,

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:17.700
these are official sources,

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:24.060
but he allegedly has become a founding member of the Indochinese Communist Party.

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:28.000
Again, we don't have other sources to back this up,

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:31.330
this is according to official backgrounds.

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:36.370
But nonetheless, he's involved.

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:43.570
He's clearly watching and very heavily influenced by these two things.

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:46.600
Number 1, the death of these very important men in his life,

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:50.995
and the other thing are the rebellions that are occurring across Indochina.

00:18:50.995 --> 00:18:53.350
This is also a time where there are

00:18:53.350 --> 00:19:00.700
the death of [inaudible] and also the arrest [inaudible],

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:08.710
two great heroes of Vietnamese nationalism of that period.

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:14.480
All of this we can imagine would have had a profound effect on top.

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:17.230
What I got to thinking was,

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:19.870
so this had this profound effect, but so what?

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:21.790
Well, as it turns out,

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:26.560
in 1930, Lê Đức Thọ happened to be at this site,

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:28.900
and it was on

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:37.780
the eve of the 13th anniversary of the Russian Revolution.

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:43.210
He's at the site along with some other friends,

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:45.760
and there is an explosion.

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:48.100
In the roundup that followed,

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:54.670
so this was around November 7th of 1930, during the roundup,

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:59.110
Lê Đức Thọ was among the 54 men,

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:00.700
I believe all men,

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:04.285
who were rounded up as suspects in the bombing.

00:20:04.285 --> 00:20:10.540
He was confirmed at this point to be himself, a communist.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:13.255
His family home was raided.

00:20:13.255 --> 00:20:16.435
They discovered a great deal of paraphernalia,

00:20:16.435 --> 00:20:18.775
that was Marxist,

00:20:18.775 --> 00:20:21.760
and he was arrested.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:26.020
The arrest, and the reason I showed you this image earlier,

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:28.705
this image is so evocative.

00:20:28.705 --> 00:20:30.580
I love this picture.

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:33.070
When he was arrested in 1930,

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:36.790
this boy was 19 years old.

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:41.005
I first saw this image when I went to Kundo Island.

00:20:41.005 --> 00:20:43.450
I don't remember when, it was a long time ago.

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:46.225
But anyway, this image is so evocative.

00:20:46.225 --> 00:20:50.000
This is a 19-year-old boy.

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:55.330
I love just looking at his eyes, his face.

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:59.860
I would ask everyone to just peer into those eyes.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:03.655
You get the sense that it looks like maybe some fear,

00:21:03.655 --> 00:21:05.410
uncertainty about the future,

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:07.615
uncertainty about what knows,

00:21:07.615 --> 00:21:12.925
but it's a really moving image.

00:21:12.925 --> 00:21:16.585
Again, this is just a 19-year-old boy.

00:21:16.585 --> 00:21:22.705
I think the first time I thought more about this image,

00:21:22.705 --> 00:21:28.120
got me to thinking about Lê Đức Thọ is more than just this one-dimensional figure,

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:35.545
opposed to people standing in Paris next to Henry Kissinger,

00:21:35.545 --> 00:21:40.120
but more just thinking about it and whenever I think of his politics,

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:43.450
whatever he did, but thinking about him as a human being.

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:46.495
Again, this is a young man who was just a teenager,

00:21:46.495 --> 00:21:48.745
a late teen, but nonetheless a teenager.

00:21:48.745 --> 00:21:55.390
Moving forward, he is going to be spending the next 5-6 years on Kundo Islands.

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:58.300
Kundo Island many of you will know it was a prison island,

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:01.915
very famous French prison island.

00:22:01.915 --> 00:22:04.645
He spent 5-6 years there.

00:22:04.645 --> 00:22:09.340
He was released in 1936 and he returned to Nam Dinh.

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:11.515
This was during the Popular Front.

00:22:11.515 --> 00:22:19.810
They released many Vietnamese political prisoners.

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:22.990
He returned to Nam Dinh.

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:28.630
What makes this an interesting time in his life is he opens a bookshop there.

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:30.610
Now, the bookshop was a front,

00:22:30.610 --> 00:22:33.205
again, for communist paraphernalia,

00:22:33.205 --> 00:22:41.215
and also to organize the apparatus of the Communist Party in the region.

00:22:41.215 --> 00:22:45.520
During this time, he became very acquainted with,

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:48.220
many of you may know the author

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:55.060
[inaudible] is most famous for writing a book called [inaudible].

00:22:55.060 --> 00:23:04.150
At any rate, [inaudible] was actually the impetus,

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:07.210
the inspiration for this story.

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:11.905
[inaudible] came in Lê Đức Thọ's bookstore.

00:23:11.905 --> 00:23:15.790
Lê Đức Thọ was working this bookstore and

00:23:15.790 --> 00:23:21.085
[inaudible] came into the bookstore one day and they start to chat.

00:23:21.085 --> 00:23:27.985
As it turns out, Lê Đức Thọ and [inaudible] were very familiar with each other because

00:23:27.985 --> 00:23:32.065
one of Lê Đức Thọ's close prison mates

00:23:32.065 --> 00:23:36.190
on Kundo Island was the younger brother of [inaudible].

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:39.100
At any rate, they became very acquainted and

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:42.760
this became a very important moment for both men.

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:51.099
This is an opportunity for Lê Đức Thọ to impress upon

00:23:51.099 --> 00:23:55.210
[inaudible] the importance of class

00:23:55.210 --> 00:24:00.850
and the importance of recognizing the international revolution,

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:07.870
and it really inspired [inaudible] three years afterwards,

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:09.070
and I want to move forward guys.

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:10.615
Three years afterward,

00:24:10.615 --> 00:24:13.610
Lê Đức Thọ was arrested again.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:20.035
This time, he was to spend the next three or four years in prison.

00:24:20.035 --> 00:24:22.690
Among them was [inaudible],

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:24.400
many of you will know that place.

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:25.780
Then up in Sangla,

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:31.190
which is up in the mountains in Northwestern Vietnam.

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.930
While he was there,

00:24:34.930 --> 00:24:44.665
he became how we might describe him,

00:24:44.665 --> 00:24:49.315
the chief of party prison operations.

00:24:49.315 --> 00:24:53.815
He was the head of the communist cell within the prison,

00:24:53.815 --> 00:24:59.275
which led to a great deal of [inaudible].

00:24:59.275 --> 00:25:03.550
Interaction with many people of many different backgrounds.

00:25:03.550 --> 00:25:06.290
Through this interaction, and

00:25:08.370 --> 00:25:12.955
I just want to leave it at this because we do need to move forward,

00:25:12.955 --> 00:25:17.740
I think one of the things that we take away from the prison experience,

00:25:17.740 --> 00:25:20.770
Number 1, it establishes his chops.

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:24.670
I think many of you will be familiar with Peter Cinnamon's work.

00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:27.625
In which Peter Cinnamon highlights how important

00:25:27.625 --> 00:25:30.850
the prison experience was to communist revolutionaries,

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:33.565
establishing their jobs as revolutionaries,

00:25:33.565 --> 00:25:39.385
and then also the communists education that they would receive.

00:25:39.385 --> 00:25:42.950
This was indeed a form of schooling.

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:46.195
The second thing that we see here,

00:25:46.195 --> 00:25:50.290
is that Lê Đức Thọ

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:57.760
demonstrates a real capacity for organization.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:00.970
Particularly in his second stint in prison,

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:04.540
he becomes the chief organizer within the prison walls.

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:10.360
In fact, he allegedly organizes a great prison break from the Con Dao Prison.

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:15.055
He himself does not escape but dozens of others do.

00:26:15.055 --> 00:26:18.915
Actually, I think I have the book right here,

00:26:18.915 --> 00:26:20.700
if any of you are interested.

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:23.055
This book just came out last year.

00:26:23.055 --> 00:26:25.065
I think many of you can see this.

00:26:25.065 --> 00:26:34.735
It's just [inaudible], the Sun Lab Prison communist cell.

00:26:34.735 --> 00:26:41.350
It's a history of the prison and the communist party within it.

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:46.524
But it demonstrates a man who was a very capable organizer,

00:26:46.524 --> 00:26:49.240
which I am going to show you in this next section.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:52.060
Again, I know we don't have that much time and I don't

00:26:52.060 --> 00:26:54.985
want to spend too much more time on this.

00:26:54.985 --> 00:26:58.550
This is where we get to the meat.

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:08.270
When Lê Đức Thọ is released from prison in 1944, 1945,

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:11.410
remarkably enough despite the fact that

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:14.680
party histories tend to not discuss this very much,

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:18.640
he's actually grown to quite a significant figure within the communist party.

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:26.080
In fact, at the time of the 1st Indochina War in 1945-1946,

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:36.640
Lê Đức Thọ has become one of the closest advisers of Ho Chi Minh in the North.

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:46.960
Here, this is an image in 1947 of Truong Chinh on the left-hand side,

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:48.655
Ho Chi Minh obviously in the middle,

00:27:48.655 --> 00:27:54.610
Vo Nguyen Giap sitting right next to Ho Chi Minh.

00:27:54.610 --> 00:27:57.835
Between Ho Chi Minh and Vo Nguyen Giap is Lê Đức Thọ.

00:27:57.835 --> 00:28:04.285
In fact, I love this picture because it almost looks like Ho Chi Minh,

00:28:04.285 --> 00:28:07.510
Truong Chinh, and Vo Nguyen Giap had set up this photograph.

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:11.140
They were going to sit together and then Lê Đức Thọ rushed over to," Hey,

00:28:11.140 --> 00:28:13.580
let me in guys. Come on, let me in".

00:28:14.880 --> 00:28:18.055
These were actually three of

00:28:18.055 --> 00:28:23.095
Ho Chi Minh's closest advisers at the beginning of the 1st Indochina War.

00:28:23.095 --> 00:28:27.070
This is something actually written about by Ban To Chu in one of

00:28:27.070 --> 00:28:33.865
his memories of the 1st Indochina War writing about Lê Đức Thọ.

00:28:33.865 --> 00:28:37.765
He describes how Lê Đức Thọ was in fact,

00:28:37.765 --> 00:28:42.490
at this point already the chief organizer of

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:45.070
the civil apparatus of

00:28:45.070 --> 00:28:49.120
the communist party versus Vo Nguyen Giap who'd have been on the military side,

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:52.000
and Truong Chinh who's the chief ideolog.

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:54.910
But Lê Đức Thọ was already of

00:28:54.910 --> 00:28:59.815
immense importance to the actual organization of the party system.

00:28:59.815 --> 00:29:05.065
However, according to the memoirs that exist,

00:29:05.065 --> 00:29:09.130
Truong Chinh and Lê Đức Thọ did not get along and they

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:13.060
often were found to be at odds with each other.

00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:18.590
By 1947, '48,

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:23.890
the two men, Truong Chinh and Lê Đức Thọ,

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:30.070
were described as two tigers [inaudible].

00:29:30.070 --> 00:29:34.765
They were described as two tigers ready to pounce on one another.

00:29:34.765 --> 00:29:42.985
It was decided that they were going to have to be separated for the sake of the party.

00:29:42.985 --> 00:29:46.570
Lê Đức Thọ, according to several memoirs,

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:51.085
had become a man with

00:29:51.085 --> 00:29:54.880
a certain description by people like

00:29:54.880 --> 00:29:59.395
[inaudible] and [inaudible] as a man who had a lust for power.

00:29:59.395 --> 00:30:05.095
In fact, there's a comment in both [inaudible] and [inaudible] books

00:30:05.095 --> 00:30:13.375
that allegedly Lê Đức Thọ would claim to some of his subordinates that he was the party.

00:30:13.375 --> 00:30:19.570
He would say, [inaudible] "I am the party,

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:21.010
the party is me".

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:22.570
Now, of course,

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:25.555
these are memoirs, and they are unofficial,

00:30:25.555 --> 00:30:32.095
and we have no other evidence for this other than what people recall.

00:30:32.095 --> 00:30:35.110
Obviously, we know that memory is fickle.

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:37.900
But it is interesting nonetheless,

00:30:37.900 --> 00:30:41.080
whatever the situation, whatever that was going on.

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:43.975
Nonetheless, in 1948,

00:30:43.975 --> 00:30:49.340
Lê Đức Thọ is ordered to head South to

00:30:52.080 --> 00:31:01.900
buttress and support the regional party apparatus in the South in the Mekong Delta.

00:31:01.900 --> 00:31:04.675
Now, as best as we can tell,

00:31:04.675 --> 00:31:07.015
Lê Đức Thọ was furious about having to leave

00:31:07.015 --> 00:31:10.120
the North and having to leave the center of the action,

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:15.040
to leave where much of

00:31:15.040 --> 00:31:18.205
the 1st Indochina War was occurring and where the seat

00:31:18.205 --> 00:31:21.925
of power for the communist party at this stage still was.

00:31:21.925 --> 00:31:26.290
However, this is a really important moment in

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:33.100
Lê Đức Thọ's life and also in the life of the party for several reasons.

00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:35.980
But key here is that

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:40.465
despite the fact that Lê Đức Thọ might have been upset about being kicked southward,

00:31:40.465 --> 00:31:43.480
about perhaps he might have seen this as expulsion,

00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:48.730
which it probably was by being sent Southward and away from the central apparatus.

00:31:48.730 --> 00:31:54.620
Nonetheless, this turned out to be a remarkable boom to him.

00:31:55.890 --> 00:31:58.375
Because of this move to South,

00:31:58.375 --> 00:31:59.935
a couple of things would happen.

00:31:59.935 --> 00:32:02.920
Number 1, he would get to work with [inaudible],

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:05.090
who we'll talk about in a second.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:11.845
Who would become later on the General Secretary of the Communist Party for over 26 years.

00:32:11.845 --> 00:32:15.940
They would work very closely for decades.

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:19.060
The second thing was because he went southward in

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:22.450
1948 and he would stay there until 1955,

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:29.320
he became insulated from the disastrous land reform that occurred in 1953,

00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:33.325
began in 1953 and it would run until 1956.

00:32:33.325 --> 00:32:39.230
Because Lê Đức Thọ went southward in 1948,

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:46.390
he was going to have the opportunity to avoid all of the stench and

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:52.870
stain of land reform.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:54.730
When he arrived South,

00:32:54.730 --> 00:33:00.265
I know today some of you have maybe read some of these books

00:33:00.265 --> 00:33:06.100
that have come out in recent years about the relationship between Lê Duẩn and Lê Đức Thọ.

00:33:06.100 --> 00:33:12.430
When he arrives in 1948 allegedly there was a meeting between Lê Đức Thọ and Lê Duẩn.

00:33:12.430 --> 00:33:16.585
A couple of scholars have discussed this at any rate.

00:33:16.585 --> 00:33:19.150
According to law,

00:33:19.150 --> 00:33:24.895
Lê Đức Thọ arrives in the South and he meets Lê Duẩn for the very first time.

00:33:24.895 --> 00:33:28.225
Lê Duẩn is a dour figure,

00:33:28.225 --> 00:33:30.250
doesn't smile a lot.

00:33:30.250 --> 00:33:35.155
Later when Lê Đức Thọ meets him for the first time, the thought was,

00:33:35.155 --> 00:33:38.185
the belief was Lê Đức Thọ was going to take over

00:33:38.185 --> 00:33:43.660
the party apparatus in the South because that's what he was sent to do.

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:46.315
However, upon meeting Lê Duẩn,

00:33:46.315 --> 00:33:48.760
according to these descriptions,

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:51.010
there have been several writers who've mentioned him and

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:53.665
I could discuss it at length later.

00:33:53.665 --> 00:33:56.350
But upon meeting Lê Duẩn,

00:33:56.350 --> 00:34:01.855
Lê Đức Thọ is so taken by the personality of Lê Duẩn.

00:34:01.855 --> 00:34:11.395
He decides that it's best that if he steps back and becomes Lê Duẩn's subordinate.

00:34:11.395 --> 00:34:15.955
This description is an interesting one,

00:34:15.955 --> 00:34:18.985
but it's really not based in fact.

00:34:18.985 --> 00:34:21.970
In fact, there's no possibility

00:34:21.970 --> 00:34:25.150
that Lê Duẩn and Lê Đức Thọ were not familiar with each other.

00:34:25.150 --> 00:34:29.080
Both of them were high-ranking party officials already, Number 1.

00:34:29.080 --> 00:34:33.265
Number 2, both had been in prison on Condore island.

00:34:33.265 --> 00:34:35.435
Whether or not they were in the same cell block,

00:34:35.435 --> 00:34:37.995
perhaps they had not met each other,

00:34:37.995 --> 00:34:40.605
but they were certainly familiar with each other.

00:34:40.605 --> 00:34:43.965
The notion that Lê Đức Thọ would not have known

00:34:43.965 --> 00:34:49.165
Lê Duẩn is really doubtful. That's number one.

00:34:49.165 --> 00:34:51.355
But the next part,

00:34:51.355 --> 00:34:53.800
the second part to this is,

00:34:53.800 --> 00:34:59.230
why might he have given away power to Lê Duẩn?

00:34:59.230 --> 00:35:02.575
Why might have he decided to become a subordinate?

00:35:02.575 --> 00:35:06.310
Well, I would argue this and I believe this to have been the case.

00:35:06.310 --> 00:35:11.830
Number 1, based on what I read of the documents that do exist and granted,

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:14.680
I need to admit here that I have not had

00:35:14.680 --> 00:35:18.010
an opportunity to look at his personal files in the archives.

00:35:18.010 --> 00:35:19.390
I've not been given permission,

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:21.980
something I would really like to do.

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:26.770
But what I surmise happens this,

00:35:26.770 --> 00:35:28.495
he arrives in the South.

00:35:28.495 --> 00:35:32.920
Lê Duẩn indeed is in charge of the regional apparatus there.

00:35:32.920 --> 00:35:39.175
But Lê Đức Thọ was a smart man and he realized several things.

00:35:39.175 --> 00:35:41.665
Number 1, he didn't have a foothold,

00:35:41.665 --> 00:35:44.290
he didn't have a place in the South yet.

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:46.345
For him to take over,

00:35:46.345 --> 00:35:51.070
the Southern Regional apparatus would have caused a great deal

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:55.780
of trouble with the local party apparatus there.

00:35:55.780 --> 00:35:58.420
That's Number 1. Number 2,

00:35:58.420 --> 00:36:06.205
Lê Đức Thọ was a really great organizer.

00:36:06.205 --> 00:36:11.560
I believe what he thought was it would be better to work

00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:19.300
from behind the throne rather than seated in it and I think that's what he did.

00:36:19.300 --> 00:36:21.100
What I mean by this,

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:22.585
he arrives in the South,

00:36:22.585 --> 00:36:26.935
he takes over the organizing of the party,

00:36:26.935 --> 00:36:33.340
he leaves the leading of the party to Lê Duẩn in the South.

00:36:33.340 --> 00:36:39.490
In so doing as the organizer,

00:36:39.490 --> 00:36:41.755
and I should explain this,

00:36:41.755 --> 00:36:51.480
several of the key aspects of organization within the party at this point is this.

00:36:51.480 --> 00:36:54.290
Number 1, he's in charge of health care.

00:36:54.290 --> 00:36:55.960
Number 2, as I said before,

00:36:55.960 --> 00:37:03.415
he's in charge of moving party members around within the apparatus.

00:37:03.415 --> 00:37:05.710
But Number 3,

00:37:05.710 --> 00:37:11.725
another thing that he becomes in-charge of, is relationships.

00:37:11.725 --> 00:37:20.020
Marriage relationships became one of his key jobs in the South.

00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:26.545
One of the first people that he arranges a marriage for happens to be Lê Duẩn.

00:37:26.545 --> 00:37:32.690
In 1949, Lê Duẩn meets a woman by the name of Nga.

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:38.095
He smitten with her and he actually mentioned Lê Đức Thọ that,

00:37:38.095 --> 00:37:44.725
oh my goodness, I've met this woman and I think I would like to marry her.

00:37:44.725 --> 00:37:47.050
Lê Duẩn already had a wife North,

00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:51.860
put that aside and he decides he wants to marry a second wife.

00:37:52.140 --> 00:37:56.755
Its Lê Đức Thọ who arranges this marriage.

00:37:56.755 --> 00:38:00.580
In party history in Vietnam,

00:38:00.580 --> 00:38:04.150
we don't really have much research done yet on this.

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:07.210
But it's interesting in Chinese studies,

00:38:07.210 --> 00:38:08.605
there has been worked on,

00:38:08.605 --> 00:38:18.370
on how the Communist Party of China used marriage as a tool to find party members,

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:20.455
allegiances to the party.

00:38:20.455 --> 00:38:23.350
Because obviously as many of you may know,

00:38:23.350 --> 00:38:26.365
this idea of arranged marriages,

00:38:26.365 --> 00:38:29.950
the person who arranges a marriage is owed

00:38:29.950 --> 00:38:34.735
a debt of gratitude from the people who are married.

00:38:34.735 --> 00:38:37.195
This is the case here.

00:38:37.195 --> 00:38:46.030
Lê Duẩn marriage is indeed arranged by Lê Đức Thọ.

00:38:46.030 --> 00:38:52.535
Yes Lê Duẩn was the superior but

00:38:52.535 --> 00:39:02.290
Lê Đức Thọ becomes the debtee,

00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:06.670
he's the person that Lê Duẩn owes a great debt to.

00:39:06.670 --> 00:39:13.075
Immediately upon this marriage and upon taking his places, the subordinate,

00:39:13.075 --> 00:39:20.350
we immediately realize the evidence and the archive shows this is that Lê Duẩn

00:39:20.350 --> 00:39:27.850
becomes almost completely trusting Lê Đức Thọ.

00:39:27.850 --> 00:39:32.830
At one point, I think I have some notes here that I've loved to put.

00:39:32.830 --> 00:39:36.590
Yes. Give me just a moment.

00:39:40.350 --> 00:39:43.940
Yes. Indeed.

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:53.520
In 1950, a party secretary from Jilin comes to Le Duan and he complains.

00:39:53.520 --> 00:39:56.355
He says, "Since Tho has come South,

00:39:56.355 --> 00:39:59.760
comrades from all over come to speak with me.

00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:03.285
I no longer see anyone talking to you."

00:40:03.285 --> 00:40:08.525
Le Duan was completely surprised by this,

00:40:08.525 --> 00:40:16.695
and he says to this provincial secretary, "What are you talking about?

00:40:16.695 --> 00:40:17.925
That's nonsense.

00:40:17.925 --> 00:40:21.135
Comrades lining up to meet with Tho is a good thing.

00:40:21.135 --> 00:40:24.645
It indicates they trust the Central Committee.

00:40:24.645 --> 00:40:30.520
What are you trying to imply by selling the name of Tho?"

00:40:31.730 --> 00:40:38.265
It's remarkable what this actually shows.

00:40:38.265 --> 00:40:43.140
It shows that Tho has ingratiated himself to Le Duan, number 1.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:51.075
Number 2, it shows actually Lê Đức Thọ's recognition of how power works,

00:40:51.075 --> 00:40:56.910
about how he can actually by standing a rank below,

00:40:56.910 --> 00:41:01.070
by not being the absolute leader,

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:02.630
but by being a lieutenant,

00:41:02.630 --> 00:41:05.600
he can actually control the flow of information,

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:11.330
which is what I argue he does subsequently when he becomes the Chief of

00:41:11.330 --> 00:41:19.360
the Central Organization Commission beginning in 1955-56.

00:41:19.360 --> 00:41:22.275
I think this is a really important moment.

00:41:22.275 --> 00:41:26.670
For me as I was reading this,

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:28.710
I realized, my goodness,

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:31.935
the way power is working here.

00:41:31.935 --> 00:41:34.665
We often think top-down,

00:41:34.665 --> 00:41:39.930
but the way it's working here is it flows downward but then upward and the

00:41:39.930 --> 00:41:45.120
amount of information that is flowing to Le Duan,

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:48.690
in this case, is determined by Lê Đức Thọ.

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:52.060
We would see this moving forward.

00:41:52.370 --> 00:41:56.010
This is Tho and Le Duan around 1950.

00:41:56.010 --> 00:42:01.035
In fact, this is the entire leadership of the Southern Regional Apparatus.

00:42:01.035 --> 00:42:04.870
You see Le Duan is on the far left.

00:42:05.390 --> 00:42:11.625
The man in the middle there in the white is Pham Hung who would later be prime minister,

00:42:11.625 --> 00:42:16.590
and Lê Đức Thọ there right next to him on the right.

00:42:16.590 --> 00:42:23.530
These are all the main members of the Southern Apparatus.

00:42:23.630 --> 00:42:28.740
I would like to move us forward another image of these men.

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:34.560
But what I want to move over to is The Architect.

00:42:34.560 --> 00:42:43.620
Lê Đức Thọ is an architect of organization in the DRV,

00:42:43.620 --> 00:42:46.455
the Democratic Republic of Vietnam in the North,

00:42:46.455 --> 00:42:49.665
his building of this organization.

00:42:49.665 --> 00:42:59.955
Lê Đức Thọ spends these 6-7 years in the South from '48 to about 1955.

00:42:59.955 --> 00:43:03.480
He returns North after the Geneva Accords.

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:05.355
Of course, as I mentioned to you,

00:43:05.355 --> 00:43:11.550
his sole journey in the South would protect him from being

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:19.365
involved with this horrible land reform project that was clearly,

00:43:19.365 --> 00:43:21.075
many of you may know,

00:43:21.075 --> 00:43:24.960
turned out to be disastrous for of the Communist Party,

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:29.355
and it led to Ho Chi Minh and [inaudible] having to

00:43:29.355 --> 00:43:36.160
apologize subsequently after 1956, 1957.

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:39.480
But Lê Đức Thọ,

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:41.940
he returns in 1955,

00:43:41.940 --> 00:43:48.480
and several of the people who've been involved in land reform are demoted.

00:43:48.480 --> 00:43:52.800
One of those who's demoted is a man who was

00:43:52.800 --> 00:43:57.180
standing in as the Chief of the Central Organization Commission.

00:43:57.180 --> 00:43:59.130
Upon his return to the North,

00:43:59.130 --> 00:44:02.085
Lê Đức Thọ is handed that position.

00:44:02.085 --> 00:44:07.260
Now, the job of the Central Organization Commission is fascinating.

00:44:07.260 --> 00:44:08.295
As I mentioned to you,

00:44:08.295 --> 00:44:10.935
he was in charge of the nomenklatura.

00:44:10.935 --> 00:44:13.200
The nomenklatura is just list of names.

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:15.840
It's basically all the Communist Party members.

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:19.695
But Lê Đức Thọ really shaped this nomenklatura

00:44:19.695 --> 00:44:23.580
and the Central Organization Commission in his own image.

00:44:23.580 --> 00:44:25.905
There's been quite a bit of research done on

00:44:25.905 --> 00:44:32.655
other Central Organization Commission's nomenclature in other communist countries.

00:44:32.655 --> 00:44:34.680
Very little done in Vietnam.

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:36.720
But I love this image here.

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:39.210
You'll notice Lê Đức Thọ is standing in the front.

00:44:39.210 --> 00:44:43.515
All of these people are members of the Central Organization Commission.

00:44:43.515 --> 00:44:47.775
This was upon Lê Đức Thọ's return from Paris.

00:44:47.775 --> 00:44:51.135
I think this was around March of 1973 maybe.

00:44:51.135 --> 00:44:54.330
He had traveled on to the Soviet Union after that,

00:44:54.330 --> 00:44:55.695
if I recall correctly.

00:44:55.695 --> 00:44:57.510
Anyway, he's returned.

00:44:57.510 --> 00:45:03.270
I love this image because it shows he's such a homely guy.

00:45:03.270 --> 00:45:05.070
He's here with his family,

00:45:05.070 --> 00:45:08.385
which are all these members of the Central Organization Commission.

00:45:08.385 --> 00:45:11.040
This doesn't look like an office that should scare us.

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:12.195
This looks like an office.

00:45:12.195 --> 00:45:17.415
They look like people you might like to have a cup of tea with, some coffee with.

00:45:17.415 --> 00:45:20.160
There are young children in the front row,

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:23.880
there's a baby being held in the third row there.

00:45:23.880 --> 00:45:28.215
This looks like a very familial oriented organization.

00:45:28.215 --> 00:45:30.750
Well, one of the great things about this image,

00:45:30.750 --> 00:45:36.930
it also is indicative of the way Lê Đức Thọ was able to feel loyalty of people.

00:45:36.930 --> 00:45:39.210
Yes, he returns North, and yes,

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:44.910
he ascends to leadership of the Central Organization Commission,

00:45:44.910 --> 00:45:47.440
but in so doing,

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:52.275
by 1956, '57,

00:45:52.275 --> 00:45:56.550
he is charged with dealing with the rectification of errors.

00:45:56.550 --> 00:45:59.805
The rectification of errors follows the land reform.

00:45:59.805 --> 00:46:07.800
It involves returning many Communist Party members

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:13.080
who had been wronged during the land reform who are renovated,

00:46:13.080 --> 00:46:15.330
they are brought back, and we're talking

00:46:15.330 --> 00:46:18.315
thousands upon thousands of Communist Party members.

00:46:18.315 --> 00:46:22.230
Well, it's Lê Đức Thọ who is charged with this.

00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:24.555
One example of this is a great story,

00:46:24.555 --> 00:46:29.730
many of you may be familiar with the very first person murdered during the land reform.

00:46:29.730 --> 00:46:31.380
Her name is [inaudible].

00:46:31.380 --> 00:46:34.470
She was this great, this wondrous figure.

00:46:34.470 --> 00:46:38.685
I know guys. Give me just a couple of moments and I'll close out.

00:46:38.685 --> 00:46:41.850
I have so much stuff and I apologize to all of you.

00:46:41.850 --> 00:46:50.400
Anyway, he's the one who saves her two sons.

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:56.280
One son was injured during the First Indochina War.

00:46:56.280 --> 00:47:02.950
The whole family was left with this big black mark on their background.

00:47:03.830 --> 00:47:10.785
Their mother, [inaudible] had been labeled a great landlord.

00:47:10.785 --> 00:47:14.550
This was a horrible label to hold.

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:17.700
The two young men who were actually soldiers,

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:21.885
her sons, suffered mightily because of it.

00:47:21.885 --> 00:47:25.650
Upon his return, he was the one who went

00:47:25.650 --> 00:47:29.745
to them and made sure that they were taken care of,

00:47:29.745 --> 00:47:32.820
and that the family was renovated and restored.

00:47:32.820 --> 00:47:37.979
[inaudible] had actually been a great help to the revolution,

00:47:37.979 --> 00:47:39.990
she had housed Ho Chi Minh and

00:47:39.990 --> 00:47:45.255
several other significant figures of the party during the First Indochina War,

00:47:45.255 --> 00:47:49.080
only to be called a landlord later on,

00:47:49.080 --> 00:47:53.445
and as I said, first person murdered during the land reform.

00:47:53.445 --> 00:47:56.910
The point of me telling the story is,

00:47:56.910 --> 00:48:03.240
it's later call who returns North towards the end of land reform,

00:48:03.240 --> 00:48:07.440
and is the person who is renovating so many of

00:48:07.440 --> 00:48:11.910
these figures who had suffered greatly because of land reform,

00:48:11.910 --> 00:48:14.505
and returns them to their place in the party.

00:48:14.505 --> 00:48:21.015
These people going forward have immense loyalty and immense feeling toward him.

00:48:21.015 --> 00:48:23.280
What I'd like to highlight right here,

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:25.770
and I know I need to move forward,

00:48:25.770 --> 00:48:27.465
just give me a moment, guys.

00:48:27.465 --> 00:48:29.220
What I'd like to highlight here,

00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:30.750
with respect to marriage,

00:48:30.750 --> 00:48:38.190
[inaudible] recognize the power that he could garner over people by arranging marriages,

00:48:38.190 --> 00:48:43.440
which he did many of including [inaudible].

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:44.790
This is a wonderful story.

00:48:44.790 --> 00:48:46.500
In fact, what's remarkable,

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:50.115
he actually got a very derisive name, later called it.

00:48:50.115 --> 00:48:51.540
The name was [inaudible].

00:48:51.540 --> 00:48:58.380
I don't know how many of you are familiar with the tail of Gil,

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:01.575
but in it, the brothel owner,

00:49:01.575 --> 00:49:10.350
her name is [inaudible] became known as [inaudible],

00:49:10.350 --> 00:49:12.915
Brother 6, the brothel owner.

00:49:12.915 --> 00:49:21.060
The reason they named him this was because he was known for arranging marriages and tris

00:49:21.060 --> 00:49:30.700
between women of the South and most all of the Northern Communist Party leadership.

00:49:30.710 --> 00:49:35.179
But anyway, this was a very derisive nickname, but nonetheless,

00:49:35.179 --> 00:49:39.980
it suggests the power that he wielded over the Communist Party.

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:42.740
I think again, we know very little about

00:49:42.740 --> 00:49:51.500
the personal relationships that men and women of the party had during this period.

00:49:51.500 --> 00:49:56.835
What we see here is giving us a window onto that.

00:49:56.835 --> 00:50:00.750
As I said, this is a way of wielding a great deal of power,

00:50:00.750 --> 00:50:04.050
and obviously another way, in this way,

00:50:04.050 --> 00:50:13.095
this ability to restore families to some form of normalcy and return them,

00:50:13.095 --> 00:50:16.425
giving them a position within the party, within the government,

00:50:16.425 --> 00:50:22.785
obviously would lend itself to giving Latex Hall

00:50:22.785 --> 00:50:29.700
a great deal of support from all these thousands of people.

00:50:29.700 --> 00:50:33.090
At any rate, he establishes,

00:50:33.090 --> 00:50:35.685
he builds a central organization commission.

00:50:35.685 --> 00:50:38.835
I just wanted to close with this.

00:50:38.835 --> 00:50:43.365
He is able to build loyalty throughout the party by this time.

00:50:43.365 --> 00:50:47.730
The Central Organization commissioned by 1960 is now in

00:50:47.730 --> 00:50:53.370
charge of literally over a million people,

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:59.610
moving people around within the party, which is significant.

00:50:59.610 --> 00:51:02.505
He's the person who decides where you go,

00:51:02.505 --> 00:51:04.170
as the head of this organization,

00:51:04.170 --> 00:51:05.700
he decides where you go.

00:51:05.700 --> 00:51:10.275
Therefore, people are beholding to him, not the party.

00:51:10.275 --> 00:51:16.000
He became the firewall for information.

00:51:16.430 --> 00:51:20.265
This is an image of him in 1960.

00:51:20.265 --> 00:51:24.690
Later on, by the Sixth Party Congress,

00:51:24.690 --> 00:51:27.570
this is 1986, this is the Doi Moi Congress.

00:51:27.570 --> 00:51:34.285
[inaudible] has gained so much power,

00:51:34.285 --> 00:51:36.440
but people within the party,

00:51:36.440 --> 00:51:45.680
[inaudible] have come to realize the figure that [inaudible],

00:51:45.680 --> 00:51:49.245
or at least they've come to believe this to be what he is,

00:51:49.245 --> 00:51:51.555
which is a Machiavellian figure.

00:51:51.555 --> 00:51:56.505
This idea of Vietnam's Machiavelli comes from this period.

00:51:56.505 --> 00:51:58.110
By the Sixth Party Congress,

00:51:58.110 --> 00:52:02.400
[inaudible] is seen as figure who has only one thing in mind,

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:04.845
which is to move pieces around.

00:52:04.845 --> 00:52:07.125
I'm sorry, I wish I had more time.

00:52:07.125 --> 00:52:10.380
I would love to talk more about the Sixth Party Congress.

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:13.305
Maybe I can save it for Q&amp;A right now,

00:52:13.305 --> 00:52:15.510
but I know I'm already way past time.

00:52:15.510 --> 00:52:17.040
Forgive me, Eric,

00:52:17.040 --> 00:52:20.115
I apologize for talking so much.

00:52:20.115 --> 00:52:22.290
I've left so much on the table,

00:52:22.290 --> 00:52:23.895
all of you, please forgive me.

00:52:23.895 --> 00:52:26.820
Jason, no need to apologize.

00:52:26.820 --> 00:52:29.985
This gives me the opportunity to invite you back.

00:52:29.985 --> 00:52:32.290
Oh, no.

00:52:32.960 --> 00:52:38.790
I've been in rapture from the very moment you started telling this story.

00:52:38.790 --> 00:52:42.720
It's really quite full of information, well-told.

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:44.595
To much I'm afraid,

00:52:44.595 --> 00:52:46.935
I apologize to all of you

00:52:46.935 --> 00:52:49.650
We'll bring you back. I think maybe we

00:52:49.650 --> 00:52:52.695
can do another talk from the Sixth Party Congress on.

00:52:52.695 --> 00:52:54.820
I would love to.

00:52:55.070 --> 00:52:58.665
What I'd like to do now is open it up to Q&amp;A.

00:52:58.665 --> 00:53:02.895
If there's anyone who has to leave at the hour or pretty soon,

00:53:02.895 --> 00:53:04.680
feel free to raise your hand first.

00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:07.890
I'll withhold my own questions for a moment.

00:53:07.890 --> 00:53:11.655
I want to make sure anyone who has an immediate question gets to go first.

00:53:11.655 --> 00:53:16.529
Just use the reaction feature,

00:53:16.529 --> 00:53:22.390
the raise hand function on Zoom and I'll call on you in order.

00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:24.885
Coy. That's all right.

00:53:24.885 --> 00:53:27.120
I see a note from Pam Coy.

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:32.640
Absolutely. Please feel free to reach out to me and I am available in Vietnam.

00:53:32.640 --> 00:53:35.355
Coy, while you're here,

00:53:35.355 --> 00:53:37.990
would you like to ask a question?

00:53:38.660 --> 00:53:42.520
Thank you Professor Picard, this is Coy.

00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:48.435
I was wondering how accessible the archives is

00:53:48.435 --> 00:53:54.390
for these figures and any other figures.

00:53:54.390 --> 00:53:57.150
Is there some censorship going?

00:53:57.150 --> 00:54:00.765
I think that's quite a broader question.

00:54:00.765 --> 00:54:04.080
Please feel free, differ that for next time.

00:54:04.080 --> 00:54:06.135
What a great question. Thank you Coy.

00:54:06.135 --> 00:54:07.935
That's great, that's a good start up.

00:54:07.935 --> 00:54:09.960
I got actually very lucky and I probably should

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:12.015
have introduced this at the beginning of what as well.

00:54:12.015 --> 00:54:21.810
I have a student who as it turns out her family is very closely related to [inaudible].

00:54:21.810 --> 00:54:23.880
Their last name is [inaudible],

00:54:23.880 --> 00:54:25.620
it used to be [inaudible].

00:54:25.620 --> 00:54:34.050
The family actually changed it due to [inaudible].

00:54:34.050 --> 00:54:37.410
At some point in the late '70s, early '80s,

00:54:37.410 --> 00:54:40.245
it became, at least for this part of the family,

00:54:40.245 --> 00:54:41.790
it become a badge of shame.

00:54:41.790 --> 00:54:45.210
But anyway, I had met with several members in the family.

00:54:45.210 --> 00:54:48.900
They were actually, I guess because she is a student of mine,

00:54:48.900 --> 00:54:52.785
they were very forthcoming in sharing material with me.

00:54:52.785 --> 00:54:56.175
However, your question Coy about the archives itself,

00:54:56.175 --> 00:54:59.340
it is really hard to get information in the archives.

00:54:59.340 --> 00:55:03.075
The personal information is almost impossible.

00:55:03.075 --> 00:55:11.055
It requires a permission from the family,

00:55:11.055 --> 00:55:13.215
not the family that I know,

00:55:13.215 --> 00:55:15.645
but the direct relatives,

00:55:15.645 --> 00:55:18.900
meaning his children, which I have not gotten.

00:55:18.900 --> 00:55:22.260
In fact, I tried to meet with his daughter,

00:55:22.260 --> 00:55:24.345
and that didn't go anywhere.

00:55:24.345 --> 00:55:28.050
It's clear they're very sensitive about that.

00:55:28.050 --> 00:55:29.415
So no, it's very hard.

00:55:29.415 --> 00:55:32.775
But with that said, memoirs exist.

00:55:32.775 --> 00:55:37.080
There's obviously a great deal of official history.

00:55:37.080 --> 00:55:43.620
Then actually [inaudible] himself has left behind actually a treasure trove.

00:55:43.620 --> 00:55:46.470
He himself did great deal of writing,

00:55:46.470 --> 00:55:50.475
he was a poet, left behind a great deal of poetry.

00:55:50.475 --> 00:55:53.085
You can actually use many newspapers,

00:55:53.085 --> 00:55:58.825
dating all the way back to the 1930s and '40s, where he appears.

00:55:58.825 --> 00:56:07.080
There is an avenue for gleaning quite a bit of information about him.

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:12.180
But no, much of the archives are still off limits.

00:56:12.180 --> 00:56:14.380
But with that said,

00:56:14.420 --> 00:56:21.000
I actually have invitation next week to visit the archives here in Hanoi,

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:22.725
which I'm really looking forward to.

00:56:22.725 --> 00:56:25.200
I've been told that I'll be able to look

00:56:25.200 --> 00:56:31.990
at some documents that may be relevant to my topic.

00:56:35.870 --> 00:56:40.095
Jason, while people are thinking of questions.

00:56:40.095 --> 00:56:43.420
I have several, but I want to start with one.

00:56:43.460 --> 00:56:50.205
You mentioned, you end with him becoming a Machiavelli figure.

00:56:50.205 --> 00:56:52.680
Obviously, you had more to say.

00:56:52.680 --> 00:56:54.550
Yes.

00:56:55.160 --> 00:56:58.770
What we got up until this point is actually

00:56:58.770 --> 00:57:04.005
a pretty congenial figure using arranged marriages of

00:57:04.005 --> 00:57:07.815
a pretty happy organizational life with

00:57:07.815 --> 00:57:13.470
that office photo that you showed towards the end and those things.

00:57:13.470 --> 00:57:17.085
In fact, many of the pictures two are quite striking,

00:57:17.085 --> 00:57:21.780
especially the one in the south where they're standing on the bridge there.

00:57:21.780 --> 00:57:26.985
The joyousness of the smiles that they have in those kinds of things.

00:57:26.985 --> 00:57:32.910
I'm curious, how do you reconcile that side with the ruthless?

00:57:32.910 --> 00:57:37.035
If you were to say, here's the ruthless side,

00:57:37.035 --> 00:57:40.230
what would be the example or two examples that

00:57:40.230 --> 00:57:44.505
really are undeniable and how do you reconcile that with that other side?

00:57:44.505 --> 00:57:47.280
Well, number one. When I first came to this,

00:57:47.280 --> 00:57:49.650
I want to admit that's a great question, Eric.

00:57:49.650 --> 00:57:51.810
In fact that this is one of the things I struggled with.

00:57:51.810 --> 00:57:55.290
When I came to it, I had already determined he was a Machiavellian figure.

00:57:55.290 --> 00:57:58.650
That's what I knew. But as I started to do this project,

00:57:58.650 --> 00:58:01.529
I became more and more sympathetic.

00:58:01.529 --> 00:58:06.130
I don't want to say that. I certainly don't agree with a lot of things he did.

00:58:08.660 --> 00:58:11.550
He clearly could be ruthless.

00:58:11.550 --> 00:58:14.730
But I want to take you back to that first picture I showed,

00:58:14.730 --> 00:58:18.010
the first image of him in prison.

00:58:19.610 --> 00:58:22.710
It is a really telling photograph.

00:58:22.710 --> 00:58:24.045
This is a 19-year-old,

00:58:24.045 --> 00:58:26.010
who's clearly just been beaten.

00:58:26.010 --> 00:58:30.089
As I said, you see some fear, some uncertainty,

00:58:30.089 --> 00:58:35.340
not having ever lived through anything like that through what he experienced,

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:44.610
I can certainly say things that he did were wrong, evil, whatever.

00:58:44.610 --> 00:58:47.160
But when you look at those eyes,

00:58:47.160 --> 00:58:52.635
when you think about what he endured, prison to wars,

00:58:52.635 --> 00:58:57.465
I think I have to admit the more and more I read,

00:58:57.465 --> 00:59:00.240
the less and less I'm more inclined to put

00:59:00.240 --> 00:59:04.755
a question mark next to Machiavelli or Machiavellian.

00:59:04.755 --> 00:59:08.760
Because part of me thinks it's not that much different

00:59:08.760 --> 00:59:13.110
than most other figures within the party at this time.

00:59:13.110 --> 00:59:16.380
This was number one, life or death.

00:59:16.380 --> 00:59:21.450
Second thing what we know about communism is it doesn't share power.

00:59:21.450 --> 00:59:26.130
I think some of these,

00:59:26.130 --> 00:59:29.205
I mentioned, he allegedly said something like,

00:59:29.205 --> 00:59:32.730
I am the party, the party is me.

00:59:32.730 --> 00:59:36.210
I think we need to look at when that came up.

00:59:36.210 --> 00:59:39.675
If you wouldn't mind can I show you another image?

00:59:39.675 --> 00:59:45.870
Sure. Again, I'm going to just go to this one.

00:59:45.870 --> 00:59:49.125
There's this image. This is towards the end of their lives.

00:59:49.125 --> 00:59:51.150
This is [inaudible] on the right,

00:59:51.150 --> 00:59:54.540
in the middle is [inaudible].

00:59:54.540 --> 00:59:56.940
Great figures. Remarkable figures.

00:59:56.940 --> 00:59:59.595
I shouldn't say great, but remarkable figures.

00:59:59.595 --> 01:00:01.650
This is at the end of their lives.

01:00:01.650 --> 01:00:04.410
For many years, these three men had at

01:00:04.410 --> 01:00:08.115
times been at odds with each other, hated each other.

01:00:08.115 --> 01:00:11.100
Or maybe hates, not the right word,

01:00:11.100 --> 01:00:17.745
but certainly had tremendous disagreements and were on

01:00:17.745 --> 01:00:25.515
opposite sides of the spectrum as it were in during the war.

01:00:25.515 --> 01:00:26.940
But towards the end of their lives,

01:00:26.940 --> 01:00:28.980
they clearly, you can see it in this image,

01:00:28.980 --> 01:00:32.190
they clearly have a real admiration for one another.

01:00:32.190 --> 01:00:35.145
This photograph was taken in 1985.

01:00:35.145 --> 01:00:41.970
This is the official photograph that often you see in books and in newspapers.

01:00:41.970 --> 01:00:45.195
But that's not the actual original photograph. This one.

01:00:45.195 --> 01:00:47.355
That's the original photograph.

01:00:47.355 --> 01:00:51.465
It's these three men and in the back, it's Lê Đức Thọ.

01:00:51.465 --> 01:00:54.150
Lê Đức, I love this photograph.

01:00:54.150 --> 01:00:56.910
Because Lê Đức Thọ was often taken out of this image,

01:00:56.910 --> 01:00:58.575
I love this photograph.

01:00:58.575 --> 01:01:00.510
It tells us so much,

01:01:00.510 --> 01:01:02.550
I think for so many,

01:01:02.550 --> 01:01:05.970
especially in the 1970s and '80s to your questionnaire,

01:01:05.970 --> 01:01:08.310
especially in the 1970s and '80s,

01:01:08.310 --> 01:01:10.680
a lot of these figures,

01:01:10.680 --> 01:01:14.145
the party itself was looking for scapegoats.

01:01:14.145 --> 01:01:17.625
One of those would ultimately become Lê Đức Thọ.

01:01:17.625 --> 01:01:24.075
Now, there is a lot of salacious material about what Lê Đức Thọ did.

01:01:24.075 --> 01:01:25.785
Some of it probably was true.

01:01:25.785 --> 01:01:30.405
For instance, many of you may be familiar,

01:01:30.405 --> 01:01:34.680
but his role in the anti-party affair of the 1960s.

01:01:34.680 --> 01:01:38.610
He is seen as the man who led

01:01:38.610 --> 01:01:43.650
the charge on behalf of Lê Duẩn because Lê Duẩn was privy to this as well.

01:01:43.650 --> 01:01:48.824
But because of Lê Đức Thọ's connection to

01:01:48.824 --> 01:01:53.790
the organization commission and therefore his connection

01:01:53.790 --> 01:01:59.865
to internal affairs and also the public security apparatus,

01:01:59.865 --> 01:02:06.075
he is regarded as the man who really organized the anti-party affair.

01:02:06.075 --> 01:02:08.910
That's number one. Number 2.

01:02:08.910 --> 01:02:12.405
Many of you may already heard this story before.

01:02:12.405 --> 01:02:14.775
Again, I want to be very clear here.

01:02:14.775 --> 01:02:16.740
I don't have any evidence for this,

01:02:16.740 --> 01:02:22.935
but one of the stories is that Lê Đức Thọ actually had Ho Chi Minh's wife,

01:02:22.935 --> 01:02:26.250
his alleged wife murdered in 1957.

01:02:26.250 --> 01:02:28.575
Again, these are salacious stories.

01:02:28.575 --> 01:02:30.810
I do not know if that's true or not.

01:02:30.810 --> 01:02:32.685
I want to be very clear here.

01:02:32.685 --> 01:02:36.165
But these are the kinds of stories that come up.

01:02:36.165 --> 01:02:38.580
I think to your point, Eric,

01:02:38.580 --> 01:02:41.670
there are a host of stories I could tell you.

01:02:41.670 --> 01:02:46.245
Whether or not they're true, I don't know.

01:02:46.245 --> 01:02:48.585
The more I hear about,

01:02:48.585 --> 01:02:50.475
the more I'm learning about Lê Đức Thọ.

01:02:50.475 --> 01:02:52.215
Again, I'm really early instance.

01:02:52.215 --> 01:02:53.595
The more and learn about him,

01:02:53.595 --> 01:02:58.320
the more I become sympathetic about what he was doing.

01:02:58.320 --> 01:03:02.100
He was clearly trying to organize a party.

01:03:02.100 --> 01:03:06.375
I see there's plenty of things I would disagree with what he did.

01:03:06.375 --> 01:03:08.955
He could be ruthless.

01:03:08.955 --> 01:03:11.955
He definitely destroyed some people's lives.

01:03:11.955 --> 01:03:15.310
I do see that. But I

01:03:16.220 --> 01:03:20.850
want to be very careful about how we present this. I think it's important.

01:03:20.850 --> 01:03:27.300
I've just recently been reading Steven Kotkin's biography of Stalin.

01:03:27.300 --> 01:03:28.650
Even there, I mean,

01:03:28.650 --> 01:03:32.640
he demonstrates a certain sensitivity.

01:03:32.640 --> 01:03:34.440
I don't know if sympathy is the right word,

01:03:34.440 --> 01:03:38.490
but certainly a certain sensitivity towards Stalin,

01:03:38.490 --> 01:03:41.670
and as brutal and horrible as he wants.

01:03:41.670 --> 01:03:43.800
I don't know if that helped Eric.

01:03:43.800 --> 01:03:45.780
If you wanted me to go further.

01:03:45.780 --> 01:03:47.010
Another question from David.

01:03:47.010 --> 01:03:50.385
But I was just thinking as you're talking, I mean,

01:03:50.385 --> 01:03:52.860
obviously you don't want to go down the path where everything's

01:03:52.860 --> 01:03:55.575
centered around American Centric narrative.

01:03:55.575 --> 01:04:01.845
But it seems to me that some of that double sidedness also appears with Henry Kissinger.

01:04:01.845 --> 01:04:06.705
It might be interesting to read some biographies of him, [inaudible] comparative stuff.

01:04:06.705 --> 01:04:10.680
But let me make sure that David can ask his question.

01:04:10.680 --> 01:04:12.735
Yeah, absolutely. Go for it, David.

01:04:12.735 --> 01:04:13.695
Go ahead, David.

01:04:13.695 --> 01:04:16.785
Yes. Thank you, Jason Picard. That was wonderful.

01:04:16.785 --> 01:04:22.620
That was just a very intriguing set of stories.

01:04:22.620 --> 01:04:27.495
I want to just ask you a little bit about my newly interest in Ho Chi Minh.

01:04:27.495 --> 01:04:33.510
I wondered if you could recommend a biography of

01:04:33.510 --> 01:04:36.360
him in English and let me know if

01:04:36.360 --> 01:04:40.620
there's other books that have not been translated that are better.

01:04:40.620 --> 01:04:43.260
Well, wow.

01:04:43.260 --> 01:04:45.300
Well, first of all, this isn't a biography.

01:04:45.300 --> 01:04:47.325
I'm going to make a plug for one of

01:04:47.325 --> 01:04:51.300
my-classmates of the University of California, Berkeley.

01:04:51.300 --> 01:04:53.775
It's not a biography, but nonetheless,

01:04:53.775 --> 01:04:59.040
Alec Holcomb's new book that just came out late last year.

01:04:59.040 --> 01:05:05.400
This year is wonderful and he does speak to Ho Chi Minh quite a bit.

01:05:05.400 --> 01:05:07.800
Alec is just a brilliant scholar,

01:05:07.800 --> 01:05:09.630
so I would first recommend that.

01:05:09.630 --> 01:05:12.840
The next thing. It's funny.

01:05:12.840 --> 01:05:16.470
Recently, I'm trying to think of anything really new and

01:05:16.470 --> 01:05:20.325
exciting that's come out in English about Ho Chi Minh.

01:05:20.325 --> 01:05:25.590
I mean still the go-to book I think is unfortunately 20 years later,

01:05:25.590 --> 01:05:28.485
William Duiker's book, Ho Chi Minh: A Life.

01:05:28.485 --> 01:05:34.960
That's still the one book that people go to that literally covers his life.

01:05:36.050 --> 01:05:39.450
Recently, I'm actually working on the translation.

01:05:39.450 --> 01:05:46.185
I'm really excited because I've been having talks with verso about the translation.

01:05:46.185 --> 01:05:49.545
But there's a book called The Winning Side,

01:05:49.545 --> 01:05:53.535
it's by a journalist here in Vietnam, Huy Duc.

01:05:53.535 --> 01:05:57.705
It's not specifically about Ho Chi Minh but it's about the Communist Party.

01:05:57.705 --> 01:06:01.380
It's a history of the Communist Party since 1975,

01:06:01.380 --> 01:06:05.025
but it does much more than just post-war history.

01:06:05.025 --> 01:06:10.920
This covers the party since its inception and it has all of these figures in it.

01:06:10.920 --> 01:06:13.965
Ho Chi Minh, [inaudible], Le Duan,

01:06:13.965 --> 01:06:18.510
Chung Ching and it's a brilliant weaving together of

01:06:18.510 --> 01:06:25.695
basically an entire history of the party since 1930.

01:06:25.695 --> 01:06:27.480
It's truly remarkable.

01:06:27.480 --> 01:06:29.625
He covers the end of the,

01:06:29.625 --> 01:06:34.095
to Erik's point, not coming from a US perspective,

01:06:34.095 --> 01:06:36.810
it starts with the collapse of the South,

01:06:36.810 --> 01:06:40.095
from a communist perspective,

01:06:40.095 --> 01:06:43.755
the arrival of the communists in the South and it moves from there

01:06:43.755 --> 01:06:47.925
into things like re-education,

01:06:47.925 --> 01:06:52.995
the boat people, Cambodia.

01:06:52.995 --> 01:06:56.745
Doi Moi, and then it talks about the Constitution.

01:06:56.745 --> 01:07:02.530
There's a chapter on the constitution that goes from 1946 all the way up to 2011.

01:07:05.420 --> 01:07:09.630
There's another chapter on literature in Vietnam,

01:07:09.630 --> 01:07:13.779
the impact of Doi Moi on literature.

01:07:13.880 --> 01:07:18.555
In that chapter, he toggles all the way back to the 1950s.

01:07:18.555 --> 01:07:26.850
It's brilliant the way he is able to bring in so many Vietnamese sources,

01:07:26.850 --> 01:07:32.580
from literature to interviews with some of the most important figures in the government,

01:07:32.580 --> 01:07:35.220
to archives, to newspapers,

01:07:35.220 --> 01:07:43.545
to even like a folk tales that are like posts 1975,

01:07:43.545 --> 01:07:47.160
folk sayings, folk tales, folk stories.

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:49.440
It's brilliant. It's really brilliant.

01:07:49.440 --> 01:07:53.790
Anyway, I would urge you hang in there in another year,

01:07:53.790 --> 01:08:02.625
I think we might have this book in translation.

01:08:02.625 --> 01:08:05.795
Yes, that's right.

01:08:05.795 --> 01:08:09.155
Yes, he did. Peter did translate that.

01:08:09.155 --> 01:08:11.995
Yes to Erik Harms, that's right.

01:08:11.995 --> 01:08:17.640
I would urge you to read Peter's review of the book,

01:08:17.640 --> 01:08:20.445
The Winning Side it's called, David.

01:08:20.445 --> 01:08:22.830
It's not yet out in English.

01:08:22.830 --> 01:08:25.170
No, it's not. But you can go online.

01:08:25.170 --> 01:08:26.745
You can actually read about it.

01:08:26.745 --> 01:08:29.715
There's quite a bit written about it in English.

01:08:29.715 --> 01:08:31.335
Great. Thank you.

01:08:31.335 --> 01:08:34.030
Yes, absolutely.

01:08:34.160 --> 01:08:37.410
David that's a really important question. You know why?

01:08:37.410 --> 01:08:38.910
Yeah, biography man,

01:08:38.910 --> 01:08:41.160
it's just not enough for Vietnam.

01:08:41.160 --> 01:08:45.960
It's sad. Sorry. Erik,

01:08:45.960 --> 01:08:47.880
did you have another question?

01:08:47.880 --> 01:08:51.100
Yeah, I have another question.

01:08:52.700 --> 01:08:59.355
I was really interested in this period in the South and you said that it ended in

01:08:59.355 --> 01:09:05.475
'55 and then it made me think about your other work,

01:09:05.475 --> 01:09:09.270
which you are working on the book manuscript which is about '54,

01:09:09.270 --> 01:09:15.000
'55 and then that led me to think of a question I'd never thought to ask before,

01:09:15.000 --> 01:09:19.950
but these party leaders who went to the South around

01:09:19.950 --> 01:09:26.280
the period right before there was going to be this big migration south,

01:09:26.280 --> 01:09:31.020
did they overlap and did they have a plan for what to do with all these

01:09:31.020 --> 01:09:36.150
Catholics coming down and how to manipulate that whole situation?

01:09:36.150 --> 01:09:40.830
I'm just curious he was there when the first migrants must have been coming.

01:09:40.830 --> 01:09:45.450
I'm wondering if you learned anything about that connection or assistance to think of it.

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:50.790
Not specifically with relationship to [inaudible].

01:09:50.790 --> 01:09:55.065
I have like 60 different memoirs relating

01:09:55.065 --> 01:10:03.180
to alleged spies who went South in 1954,

01:10:03.180 --> 01:10:06.915
'55 with those migrants to stir up trouble.

01:10:06.915 --> 01:10:12.810
You mentioned one of my papers in which I talk about actually may not have mentioned,

01:10:12.810 --> 01:10:16.965
but I wrote a paper that starts with

01:10:16.965 --> 01:10:25.905
the great Pho scare of 1955.

01:10:25.905 --> 01:10:28.005
In March of 1955,

01:10:28.005 --> 01:10:36.180
there's a rumor that spreading all over Saigon in which allegedly

01:10:36.180 --> 01:10:40.905
northern migrants are abducting young children to

01:10:40.905 --> 01:10:46.020
harvest their bodies for meat that they're going to put in the Pho.

01:10:46.020 --> 01:10:50.160
Sorry, forgive me everyone for giving such a description.

01:10:50.160 --> 01:10:52.920
But this was quite serious.

01:10:52.920 --> 01:10:58.470
It was in all of the newspapers in Saigon and in fact, at that point,

01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:04.590
[inaudible] and the mayor of Saigon had to come out and allay people's fears.

01:11:04.590 --> 01:11:05.910
You know, this wasn't true.

01:11:05.910 --> 01:11:07.770
There is no evidence for this.

01:11:07.770 --> 01:11:09.840
I have seen no evidence for this.

01:11:09.840 --> 01:11:14.460
But this was clearly a roost that was cooked

01:11:14.460 --> 01:11:20.400
up by communist spies,

01:11:20.400 --> 01:11:22.800
but as I argue, there is clearly

01:11:22.800 --> 01:11:26.805
something in the minds of Southerners that would allow them to believe that.

01:11:26.805 --> 01:11:29.295
That's a question for another day.

01:11:29.295 --> 01:11:34.950
But I have host of these memoirs of spies who come South in '54,

01:11:34.950 --> 01:11:40.830
'55, and they are certainly causing trouble.

01:11:40.830 --> 01:11:44.610
One other story and this is connected to Lê Đức Thọ which returns

01:11:44.610 --> 01:11:49.560
North in early 1954 on a Polish ship,

01:11:49.560 --> 01:11:51.990
the Kolinsky, I believe it's called.

01:11:51.990 --> 01:11:57.540
Interestingly enough, a couple of weeks after he arrives in the North,

01:11:57.540 --> 01:12:00.420
that ship returns South.

01:12:00.420 --> 01:12:07.050
It's one of the few non-French and American ships to carry passengers South.

01:12:07.050 --> 01:12:08.625
It returns South.

01:12:08.625 --> 01:12:15.010
The manifest claims that it was supposed to have about 3,000 people aboard.

01:12:15.350 --> 01:12:19.980
However, it arrives with almost 3,500 people aboard.

01:12:19.980 --> 01:12:24.180
Because of that, the [inaudible] government sent all of these people to

01:12:24.180 --> 01:12:29.190
Phuc walk and had them in detention for several months.

01:12:29.190 --> 01:12:34.830
Clearly this was something that was drummed up by the communists in the North.

01:12:34.830 --> 01:12:39.520
They did this, they knew what was going to happen.

01:12:39.770 --> 01:12:42.900
They did this for a purpose and obviously,

01:12:42.900 --> 01:12:45.220
as I said this was a Polish ship.

01:12:46.160 --> 01:12:54.240
There may have been some involvement of international communism as well in that.

